WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.458 --> 00:00:04.212
Oh. You think darkness 
is your ally?

00:00:04.754 --> 00:00:06.798
You merely adopted the dark.

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.551
I was born in it.

00:00:09.551 --> 00:00:11.136
Molded by it.

00:00:11.136 --> 00:00:12.053
Ahem.

00:00:12.377 --> 00:00:16.599
That's my impression of Bane in
2012's "The Dark Knight Rises"

00:00:16.599 --> 00:00:18.226
when he's chastising Batman

00:00:18.226 --> 00:00:20.228
for using the dark 
to his advantage.

00:00:20.228 --> 00:00:23.606
Now my impression sucks, but 
there's arguably no character

00:00:23.606 --> 00:00:26.484
more associated with the movie 
darkness than Batman.

00:00:27.068 --> 00:00:30.196
With 13 on screen appearances 
across nearly 60 years

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:33.074
Batman is one of the longest 
running franchises.

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:35.994
And when you take films from 
across different eras

00:00:35.994 --> 00:00:39.330
and compress them into one 
big barcode looking thing

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:41.207
they can serve as a sort of proxy

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:43.752
for a story about the 
darkening of movies

00:00:43.752 --> 00:00:46.004
and TV in general.

00:00:46.004 --> 00:00:50.467
In the 1960s, Batman movies burst 
with color and brightness

00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:52.719
but by 2022's "The Batman"...

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:54.804
I mean, just look at it.

00:00:54.804 --> 00:00:57.724
It's a movie that lives 
and breathes darkness.

00:00:58.016 --> 00:01:00.518
So much darkness that 
some audience members

00:01:00.518 --> 00:01:02.771
have complained that it's almost too dark.

00:01:03.313 --> 00:01:05.940
You can see the same pattern 
in the Harry Potter films...

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:08.485
and in Game of Thrones...

00:01:09.194 --> 00:01:11.738
and the Spider-Man franchises.

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:14.949
So, yeah, it's not just you complaining.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.787
Movies and TV shows 
are getting darker

00:01:18.787 --> 00:01:21.790
and there are a few reasons why.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.797
Check out this graph by Stephen Follows

00:01:28.797 --> 00:01:30.965
a data researcher for the film industry.

00:01:31.216 --> 00:01:36.346
He charted the top 100 grossing films 
in the U.S. from 2000 to 2015

00:01:36.346 --> 00:01:38.056
and what type of camera they were shot on.

00:01:38.473 --> 00:01:39.766
This point here.

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:42.852
It's where movies flipped
from film to digital.

00:01:42.852 --> 00:01:45.480
And this shift from 
 film to digital

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.982
is part of the reason 
for this shift.

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:49.734
The biggest difference

00:01:49.734 --> 00:01:53.738
between shooting dark scenes 
on film versus digital

00:01:53.738 --> 00:01:55.949
is not knowing exactly 
what you have.

00:01:56.032 --> 00:01:57.242
My name is Brett Jutkiewicz.

00:01:57.242 --> 00:01:58.952
I'm a cinematographer.

00:01:58.952 --> 00:02:02.497
Some of my recent work includes 
"Ready or Not"

00:02:02.497 --> 00:02:05.834
"The Black Phone", "Scream"
and "Stranger Things" season four.

00:02:07.001 --> 00:02:08.586
When shooting a movie on film,

00:02:08.586 --> 00:02:10.630
there is way less room for error

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:13.049
and way more opportunities 
to make mistakes.

00:02:13.174 --> 00:02:16.886
For one, there are no monitors that'll 
show filmmakers exactly

00:02:16.886 --> 00:02:19.472
what a shot is going to look like
before they are recorded.

00:02:19.722 --> 00:02:22.308
There are video taps 
for film cameras

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:24.769
but they're really just a reference 
for framing.

00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:26.771
It's really not representative

00:02:26.771 --> 00:02:28.982
of how the image will actually look.

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:31.526
It's using a light meter and 

00:02:31.526 --> 00:02:33.611
measuring the light in 
the scene and 

00:02:33.611 --> 00:02:35.864
trusting that you're getting 
what you want.

00:02:35.864 --> 00:02:38.199
If you do shoot a dark scene 
on film 

00:02:38.199 --> 00:02:40.493
there isn't much room 
to correct it later

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:43.121
because the information in the 
darkest parts of the image

00:02:43.121 --> 00:02:44.831
is essentially lost.

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:47.959
The overall image just becomes grainier.

00:02:48.459 --> 00:02:51.254
And in Hollywood, there's 
little room for error.

00:02:52.213 --> 00:02:54.257
You really can't do 
a scene again

00:02:54.257 --> 00:02:57.010
just paying for the day, 
the location.

00:02:57.177 --> 00:02:59.137
There's really no room for going back

00:02:59.137 --> 00:03:01.806
and redoing something if 
it comes out too dark

00:03:01.806 --> 00:03:04.642
or if it's not exactly what you want.

00:03:04.642 --> 00:03:06.269
Take a look at these two shots.

00:03:06.895 --> 00:03:10.815
This one from 2002's "Spider-Man" 
was shot on film.

00:03:10.815 --> 00:03:13.359
This one from 2012's 
"The Amazing Spider-Man" 

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:15.111
was shot on digital.

00:03:15.111 --> 00:03:16.070
See how much brighter 

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:18.781
Spider-Man and Mary Jane's 
faces are?

00:03:18.781 --> 00:03:22.619
With film, the safest bet is to shoot 
with plenty of light

00:03:22.619 --> 00:03:24.954
even if scenes are meant to be dark.

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:26.915
Shooting digitally allows you

00:03:26.915 --> 00:03:31.044
to take more chances with darkness 
because digital shoots

00:03:31.044 --> 00:03:33.796
usually have high-quality 
digital monitors.

00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:37.175
The way that it allows people 
to see the image

00:03:37.175 --> 00:03:39.219
as it will be when it's finished

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:41.804
lends itself to taking more risks.

00:03:42.013 --> 00:03:45.600
Plus, digital cameras are generally 
more sensitive to light.

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:48.436
They do capture more into the highlights

00:03:48.436 --> 00:03:51.272
and more into the shadows 
than a film stock can.

00:03:51.606 --> 00:03:52.565
Digital cameras

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:54.817
like the one we're using 
to film this right now

00:03:54.817 --> 00:03:56.986
shoot in what's called 
a log format.

00:03:57.362 --> 00:03:59.697
This essentially is when 
the camera sensor

00:03:59.697 --> 00:04:02.617
is capturing a really flat, 
grayish image

00:04:02.617 --> 00:04:05.787
in order to preserve information
in the highlights and the shadows.

00:04:06.037 --> 00:04:08.873
Once you bring that footage into
a color grading software

00:04:08.873 --> 00:04:11.918
you have tons of room to push 
that footage around.

00:04:12.252 --> 00:04:14.128
And instead of losing detail

00:04:14.128 --> 00:04:16.965
in the darkest parts of the image 
like you would with film

00:04:16.965 --> 00:04:20.802
you can push it way brighter and 
still retain that detail.

00:04:21.094 --> 00:04:24.347
And the overall image doesn't 
get super grainy either.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.850
Now, I know what you're thinking.

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:31.396
Lots of very successful dark scenes 
have been shot on film

00:04:31.396 --> 00:04:34.357
from "The Godfather" to "The Dark Knight" 
to "Euphoria".

00:04:34.857 --> 00:04:37.652
It's not impossible to do 
dark scenes on film.

00:04:38.319 --> 00:04:40.989
It's just harder and riskier.

00:04:40.989 --> 00:04:43.199
Regardless of film or digital.

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:45.285
Shooting dark scenes is always a balance

00:04:45.285 --> 00:04:48.413
between information and 
artistic intentions.

00:04:48.788 --> 00:04:50.915
There was an episode 
in "Severance".

00:04:50.915 --> 00:04:54.669
There's shots in that sequence 
where you really are

00:04:54.669 --> 00:04:57.672
only seeing just a sliver of his face.

00:04:57.672 --> 00:05:01.592
And this sea of darkness and watching it,
I was struck by it.

00:05:02.051 --> 00:05:03.886
There's instances

00:05:03.886 --> 00:05:08.641
where you really want to focus 
on one thing in the frame

00:05:08.641 --> 00:05:10.601
and using darkness as a tool

00:05:10.601 --> 00:05:13.062
to kind of guide the eye 
in that way.

00:05:13.313 --> 00:05:15.940
Darkness only feels like a problem

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:18.735
when it feels like we're missing out 
on visual information

00:05:18.735 --> 00:05:20.903
that we think we're supposed to see.

00:05:20.903 --> 00:05:22.822
It's tricky because at the same time

00:05:22.822 --> 00:05:25.950
as you want to create a mood 
and a feeling for the audience...

00:05:26.409 --> 00:05:29.245
you don't want to also get 
in the way of them 

00:05:29.245 --> 00:05:31.039
enjoying the show.

00:05:31.039 --> 00:05:32.665
It's a balance.

00:05:32.707 --> 00:05:34.876
Filmmakers are using the best screens

00:05:34.876 --> 00:05:38.046
the world has to offer in  
perfectly dark rooms

00:05:38.046 --> 00:05:40.923
when tweaking the brightness of the content
that we're all watching.

00:05:41.299 --> 00:05:44.802
So filmmakers choose to make something 
look right on the best screen

00:05:44.802 --> 00:05:48.765
and they expect that vision to trickle down
to whatever screen

00:05:48.765 --> 00:05:51.309
you end up watching it on 
later on down the line.

00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:53.436
It's impossible to know

00:05:53.436 --> 00:05:55.772
how people are going to watch it in the end.

00:05:55.772 --> 00:05:59.817
And if you make it look good on 
a low quality television

00:05:59.817 --> 00:06:02.153
then when people are 
in the theater

00:06:02.153 --> 00:06:04.238
it's not going to look 
right for them.

00:06:04.489 --> 00:06:07.116
So, yes, what you're watching is dark...

00:06:07.617 --> 00:06:11.204
but it's also exactly what the filmmaker 
 wanted you to see.

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:16.209
There's hope that movie darkness 
is still evolving for the better.

00:06:16.584 --> 00:06:19.420
"Ad Astra" and "Nope" were filmed 
using regular cameras

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:21.547
and infrared cameras.

00:06:21.547 --> 00:06:24.759
Infrared cameras allow filmmakers 
to essentially steal light

00:06:24.759 --> 00:06:28.346
from the sections of the light spectrum
that are invisible to the human eye.

00:06:29.013 --> 00:06:32.433
It also creates a black sky,
which is useful for compositing.

00:06:32.975 --> 00:06:34.685
When blending the two images together

00:06:34.685 --> 00:06:36.229
it can produce a darkness

00:06:36.229 --> 00:06:38.231
that allows you to peer much deeper

00:06:38.231 --> 00:06:41.025
into the dark expanse than 
normal movie darkness would.

